Below is a compact, self‑contained Java program that demonstrates the most common ways you’ll work with dates using the `java.time` API (Java 8+).  
It shows parsing/formatting, simple arithmetic, periods, durations, time‑zones, and how to use a `Clock` to make the code testable.

```java
import java.time.*;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;
import java.time.temporal.ChronoUnit;

public class DateDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 1️⃣  Working with a simple calendar date (no time component)
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        LocalDate today = LocalDate.now();                       // 2026‑10‑09 (example)
        System.out.println("Today (LocalDate)          : " + today);

        // Add / subtract days, weeks, months
        LocalDate nextWeek   = today.plusWeeks(1);
        LocalDate lastMonth  = today.minusMonths(1);
        System.out.println("Next week (LocalDate)       : " + nextWeek);
        System.out.println("One month ago (LocalDate)   : " + lastMonth);

        // Parse a date string
        DateTimeFormatter fmt = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-MM-yyyy");
        LocalDate parsed = LocalDate.parse("31-12-2023", fmt);
        System.out.println("Parsed date (31‑12‑2023)    : " + parsed);

        // Format a date to string
        String formatted = parsed.format(fmt);
        System.out.println("Formatted back to string   : " + formatted);

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 2️⃣  Working with a date‑time (with time component)
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        LocalDateTime nowLDT = LocalDateTime.now();               // current date‑time
        System.out.println("\nNow (LocalDateTime)       : " + nowLDT);

        // Add hours / minutes
        LocalDateTime later = nowLDT.plusHours(2).plusMinutes(30);
        System.out.println("In 2h30m (LocalDateTime)   : " + later);

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 3️⃣  Working with time‑zones
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        ZonedDateTime utcNow = ZonedDateTime.now(ZoneOffset.UTC);
        System.out.println("\nNow (ZonedDateTime UTC)   : " + utcNow);

        ZonedDateTime seoulNow = utcNow.withZoneSameInstant(ZoneId.of("Asia/Seoul"));
        System.out.println("Now in Seoul              : " + seoulNow);

        // Convert back to Instant (instant on the global timeline)
        Instant instant = utcNow.toInstant();
        System.out.println("Instant (same moment)      : " + instant);

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 4️⃣  Periods and Durations
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // Period (date‑based amount of time)
        LocalDate birthDate = LocalDate.of(1990, Month.APRIL, 15);
        Period agePeriod = Period.between(birthDate, today);
        System.out.println("\nAge (Period)               : " + agePeriod.getYears() + "y " 
                + agePeriod.getMonths() + "m " + agePeriod.getDays() + "d");

        // Duration (time‑based amount of time)
        Duration duration = Duration.between(nowLDT, later);
        System.out.println("Duration between now & later: " + duration.toHours() + "h "
                + duration.toMinutesPart() + "m");

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 5️⃣  ChronoUnit (one‑step calculations)
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        long daysBetween = ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(today, nextWeek);
        System.out.println("\nDays between today and next week: " + daysBetween);

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 6️⃣  Using Clock for testable code
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        Clock fixedClock = Clock.fixed(Instant.parse("2026-10-09T00:00:00Z"), ZoneId.systemDefault());
        LocalDate todayFromFixed = LocalDate.now(fixedClock);
        System.out.println("\nToday from fixed Clock       : " + todayFromFixed);

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 7️⃣  Iterate over a range of dates
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        System.out.println("\nDates in the current month:");
        LocalDate startOfMonth = today.withDayOfMonth(1);
        LocalDate endOfMonth   = today.withDayOfMonth(today.lengthOfMonth());

        for (LocalDate d = startOfMonth; !d.isAfter(endOfMonth); d = d.plusDays(1)) {
            System.out.println(d);
        }
    }
}
```

### What this demo covers

| Feature | Code snippet | What it shows |
|---------|--------------|---------------|
| `LocalDate` | `LocalDate.now()` | Current calendar date, adding/subtracting days, months, parsing & formatting |
| `LocalDateTime` | `LocalDateTime.now()` | Current date‑time, adding hours & minutes |
| `ZonedDateTime` | `ZonedDateTime.now(ZoneOffset.UTC)` | Time‑zone aware date‑time, converting zones, to `Instant` |
| `Period` | `Period.between(birthDate, today)` | Date‑based duration (years, months, days) |
| `Duration` | `Duration.between(nowLDT, later)` | Time‑based duration (hours, minutes) |
| `ChronoUnit` | `ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(today, nextWeek)` | One‑step calculations |
| `Clock` | `Clock.fixed(...)` | Testable date logic |
| Date range iteration | `for (LocalDate d = start; !d.isAfter(end); d = d.plusDays(1))` | Iterate over every day in a month |

Compile and run this file (`javac DateDemo.java && java DateDemo`) to see the output. Feel free to tweak the dates and time‑zones to experiment further!
